put(7,'Abstract. Satellite land surface temperature (LST) combines vegetation and soil contributions, limiting its interpretation over heterogeneous surfaces. LS4T-MSG describes hourly vegetation-canopy and background-soil temperatures as monthly means from paired Meteosat Second Generation observations over Europe and Africa during August 2017–December 2025. The retrieval targets daytime conditions with pronounced canopy–soil thermal contrast, with observations sampled at seven nominal UTC hours (10:00–16:00) on a nominal 0.03° grid. A constrained sequential neighborhood estimate supplies the target for a regularized joint adjustment of canopy, sunlit-soil and shaded-soil radiances. Background-soil temperature is obtained by combining soil radiances and applying the inverse Planck function. Station evaluation uses monthly means calculated separately at 11:00–16:00 UTC. At Évora, sunlit-soil and canopy RMSEs are 1.883 and 3.427 K across 30 month–hour pairs during August–December 2017; the background-soil comparison gives 2.929 K. At 12 European stations during 2018–2024, mean station RMSEs are 4.014 K for shaded soil against soil probes and 3.626 K for canopy against air temperature. These comparisons are conditional on the retained observations and describe station retrievals, with the European measurements providing indirect references. The distinction between station evaluation and the earlier regional fields limits transfer of these scores to the complete dataset. Monthly stratification by hour preserves information on mean diurnal temperature contrasts that is lost in a single monthly mean.')
put(22,'LST and LAI are uncertain inputs: residual cloud contamination, atmospheric correction, emissivity, calibration and cross-sensor differences affect both observations and component contributions. The retrieval pairs identical EURO and IODC timestamps, uses daily EURO LAI, and applies common input and output validity criteria (Table 2). Figure 2 illustrates the directional contrast between the two LST observations at 13:00 UTC on 15 August 2018. Across 2,323,118 jointly valid pixels within 250–350 K, the EURO–IODC difference has a mean of 1.10 K and a standard deviation of 2.61 K, without difference-based exclusion. A small mean difference can conceal compensating errors and does not imply unbiased component retrievals.')
put(24,'Figure 2. MSG-EURO minus MSG-IODC LST at 13:00 UTC on 15 August 2018. The map uses a −15 to +15 K display scale; values outside this range remain in the statistics and complete histogram. Differences reflect directional effects together with residual cross-sensor, atmospheric and sampling differences.')
put(26,p[26].text.replace('Ancillary source identifiers are recorded in the reproduction-package README. ','').replace('Their processing provenance, spectral representativeness and temporal mismatch','Spectral representativeness and temporal mismatch'))
put(28,p[28].text.replace('The evaluated estimates are calculated directly from cached station inputs rather than extracted from the regional monthly grids (Table 2).','The evaluated temperatures are retrieved directly from satellite observations around each station rather than extracted from the regional monthly grids.').replace(' CD-Ygb is outside this European evaluation and appears only in the earlier numerical replay tests of Sect. 4.4.',''))
put(39,'Figure 3. Schematic retrieval workflow for the earlier regional configuration. The station evaluation uses the parameter settings in Table 2, with 5 × 5 neighborhoods at both stages and λ = 1000.')
# Restore exactly the earlier flowchart requested by the author.
oldfig=Path(r'C:\work\ls4t-msg\cases\official\results\figure_reproduction\figures\Figure_03.png')
assert oldfig.exists()
p[38].clear();p[38].add_run().add_picture(str(oldfig),width=Inches(6.65));p[38].alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER;p[38].paragraph_format.keep_with_next=True
put(59,'The isotropic form uses C_D = σ²I₂ and C_M = (σ²/λ)I₃, giving m = (WᵀW + λI₃)⁻¹(WᵀL + λmₚ). Here σ² has squared-radiance units and λ is a dimensionless relative penalty; the common σ² factor cancels from this point estimate. The station evaluation uses λ = 1000, hotspot parameter 0.25, δ = 3 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹ and 5 × 5 windows at both stages (Table 2). For a singular value s of W, the residual-to-update gain is s/(s² + λ), illustrating the influence of regularization when angular information is weak. After the joint adjustment, shaded-soil radiance is capped at sunlit-soil radiance; the constrained state therefore need not minimize the unconstrained objective in Eq. (6).')
put(60,'3.3 Monthly aggregation and dataset characteristics')
put(61,'The dataset is organized by calendar month and nominal UTC hour from 10:00 to 16:00 over August 2017–December 2025. Available observations begin on 3 August 2017, making the first month partial. The primary variables are vegetation-canopy and background-soil temperature, with sunlit and shaded soil retained as diagnostic components. Background-soil temperature is defined at the retrieval stage by:')
put(63,'Here pₛ = exp[−0.5 LAI / cos(θₛ)] is the canopy gap probability in the solar direction, where θₛ is solar zenith angle. The weights describe ground illumination rather than a satellite reference view. Material emissivity cancels because the soil states share the same emissivity. For positive LAI and 0 ≤ θₛ < 90°, pₛ lies between 0 and 1; this daylight condition is applied in the station evaluation. Equation (8) combines radiances before converting to temperature, rather than averaging the two soil temperatures arithmetically.')
put(64,'The regional fields use a nominal 0.03° WGS 84 geographical grid spanning 18° W–61° E and 35° S–65° N, with 3333 rows and 2633 columns. MSG footprints are approximately 3 km at nadir and increase away from nadir; neighborhood pooling further broadens the effective spatial support. Each monthly HDF5 field contains background-soil, canopy, sunlit-soil and shaded-soil temperatures, in that order. Values are stored as kelvin multiplied by 100, with NaN representing missing data.')
put(65,'Monthly means are calculated separately for each grid cell, calendar month and UTC hour from available valid temperatures after radiance-to-temperature conversion. They represent clear-sky conditions at the specified hour rather than daily or all-weather monthly means. Sampling varies with cloud cover, vegetation screening and joint availability of the two views. Consequently, complete month–hour coverage does not imply uniform temporal support at every pixel. The earlier regional fields used for the spatial illustrations differ from the station configuration in Table 2 and are interpreted separately from the station accuracy estimates.')
put(66,'Table 2. Retrieval parameters and validity criteria used in the station evaluation.')
t=d.tables[1]
while len(t.rows)>7:t._tbl.remove(t.rows[-1]._tr)
values=[['Parameter or criterion','Setting','Role'],['Canopy structure','G = 0.5; clumping index = 1; hotspot = 0.25','Controls directional component fractions'],['Neighborhoods','5 × 5 at both stages; ≥10 valid paired neighbors','Local component-radiance estimation'],['Regularization','λ = 1000; δ = 3 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹','Balances the joint adjustment and sequential constraints'],['Input validity','Center LAI ≥ 0.05; 0 ≤ solar and viewing zenith angles < 90°','Retains vegetated pixels with valid illumination and viewing geometry'],['Temperature validity','All four components finite and within 250–350 K','Excludes invalid retrievals before reference-based evaluation'],['Evaluation support','11:00–16:00 UTC; ≥3 instantaneous pairs per month–hour; ≥3 groups per European station','Preserves monthly and hourly structure on paired samples']]
