put(79,p[79].text.replace('corrected station configuration','station configuration').replace('The source EVO CSV contains no timezone declaration; this time convention remains an assumption of the current matching.','The observation timestamps are assumed to be UTC because an explicit source-time convention is unavailable.'))
put(87,p[87].text.replace('corrected station-mode predictions','station retrievals').replace('The station configuration is identical to that used at EVO: hotspot parameter 0.25, λ = 1000, δ = 3 W m⁻² sr⁻¹ μm⁻¹ and 5 × 5 windows at both stages.','The retrieval configuration is the same as at EVO (Table 2).'))
put(88,'The meteorological observations are reported over 30-min intervals. Satellite observations are matched to the nearest interval start within ±30 min, assuming UTC timestamps. This unverified source-time assumption contributes uncertainty to the hourly comparisons. The principal evaluation uses 11:00–16:00 UTC to align the two validation domains. Sensitivity to the 12-site selection is discussed in Sect. 5.1.')
for i in range(91,95):delete(i)
put(97,'The station comparisons retain monthly and hourly structure while sampling different periods and reference quantities at EVO and in Europe (Sect. 2.4). Including IT-TrF and FR-EM2 gives 14-station mean RMSEs of 3.957 K for shaded soil and 3.854 K for canopy, compared with 4.014 and 3.626 K for the post hoc 12-site subset. Removing the two sites therefore does not improve both components. Unequal sample support also affects interpretation: pooling all retained month–hour pairs gives RMSEs of 4.032 and 4.267 K, whereas the principal statistics weight stations equally. Leaving out each station in turn gives mean RMSE ranges of 3.952–4.055 K for soil and 3.492–3.818 K for canopy. These ranges describe sample sensitivity rather than statistical confidence intervals.')
put(99,'Sensitivity analysis considered hotspot values of 0.10, 0.20, 0.25 and 0.30 and neighborhood widths of three or five pixels at each stage, with λ and δ held fixed. Lower errors on a configuration’s own retained samples did not necessarily persist on common samples. For example, hotspot = 0.30 with 5 × 5 and 3 × 3 neighborhoods yielded soil/canopy RMSEs of 3.835/3.575 K on its own support, but on shared support soil RMSE was 0.151 K higher and canopy RMSE 0.045 K lower than for the adopted configuration. The experiment indicates a tradeoff between components and sampling, rather than a uniformly superior parameter combination.')
put(100,'Retrospective temporal comparisons also indicate variable support. European soil/canopy RMSEs were 3.951/4.021 K during 2018–2022 and 3.992/3.126 K during 2023–2024. Nine stations met the minimum support in each period, but their composition differed. Because both periods had previously been examined, these differences cannot establish independent predictive skill or a temporal improvement in retrieval performance.')
put(104,'Pixel-specific slopes in Fig. 10 account for observed calendar-month effects and a June 2022 step. Both components must be jointly valid in at least 60 of 96 months, with at least three months in every year and eight calendar months each represented by four or more observations. These criteria retain 1,340,385 pixels while allowing recurring seasonal gaps. Slopes are fitted at the actual valid timestamps without gap filling and therefore describe available seasons and clear-sky samples. Their spatial support differs from Fig. 9, and no pixel-level significance or multiple-testing inference is made.')
put(106,p[106].text.replace(' The stricter 407,557-pixel support is recorded for sensitivity checks; no significance or attribution claim is made.',' No significance or attribution claim is made.'))
put(111,'The main limitations concern component identifiability, the representativeness of validation references, and the spatial and temporal support of the observations.')
put(112,p[112].text.replace('The hotspot and window experiment characterizes selected configuration changes (Appendix A), but does not quantify uncertainty from LST, LAI, emissivity or geometry perturbations.','Parameter sensitivity alone does not quantify uncertainty arising from LST, LAI, emissivity and viewing geometry.'))
put(113,'Coarse satellite footprints can include irrigation, water, urban surfaces and heterogeneous vegetation that are incompletely represented by a canopy–soil model. Direct component observations are limited to the 2017 EVO open-woodland case, whereas the European comparisons use soil-probe and air temperatures (Sect. 2.4). Unknown measurement depths and heights, assumed timestamp conventions and sparse support at some sites constrain broader accuracy claims. The station statistics assess local retrievals rather than temperatures extracted from the regional monthly grids.')
put(114,'The regional illustrations use an earlier configuration (hotspot = 0.2, λ = 100 and sequential 3 × 3 and 5 × 5 neighborhoods). Differences in low-temperature input handling, daylight screening and viewing geometry further limit their comparability with the station evaluation. Figures 4, 9 and 10 therefore provide illustrative spatial and temporal patterns rather than validation of the adopted configuration. The IODC satellite transition in June 2022 must be considered when interpreting interannual differences; a fitted transition term cannot establish observational homogeneity.')
put(115,'Monthly sampling follows joint clear-sky availability, and fixed UTC hours correspond to different local solar times across longitude. The partial 2017 record and changing valid-pixel support limit interannual comparisons. Per-pixel sampling information and uncertainty estimates would improve interpretation, while additional independent component-radiometry sites are needed to assess transfer across land-cover types and climate regimes.')
put(117,'LS4T-MSG represents vegetation-canopy and background-soil temperatures as monthly means at individual hours over Europe and Africa during August 2017–December 2025. The station evaluation preserves this temporal structure by comparing paired monthly means separately at 11:00–16:00 UTC. EVO sunlit-soil and canopy RMSEs are 1.883 and 3.427 K across 30 month–hour pairs; the background-soil comparison gives 2.929 K. At the 12 European stations, equal-station mean RMSEs are 4.014 K for shaded soil against soil probes and 3.626 K for canopy against air temperature. These estimates characterize the retained station samples and their respective reference quantities.')
put(118,'Monthly stratification by hour retains information on mean diurnal canopy–soil thermal contrasts beyond a single monthly temperature. The direct EVO and indirect European comparisons offer complementary evidence, while parameter and sample sensitivity emphasize the dependence of retrieval performance on observational support. Transfer to regional accuracy requires consistent retrieval settings, representative references and verified temporal alignment. The regional examples illustrate variability and do not establish climate trends.')
